## Greenhouse

**What it is:** Greenhouse is an ATS built around structured hiring. Companies use it to create scorecards, interview plans, and consistent evaluation processes.

**What it feels like as a candidate:** A fairly clean application page with fields for your details, resume, optional cover letter, links, and a set of questions the employer wrote. Many applicants do not need to create an account.

**Where you will see it:** Technology startups, scale-ups, and many mid-size companies.

**Strengths for candidates:**

- Quick to complete
- Straightforward layout
- Custom questions let you show personality and thinking

**Watch out for:**

- Custom essay-style questions can take more time than the form suggests
- Some roles include multiple written prompts, so budget time accordingly

**How to win:** Spend your effort on the custom questions. Because the form itself is simple, the quality of your written answers is often what separates applicants.

You can browse [Greenhouse jobs on GetHired](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/jobs?platform=greenhouse).

## Lever

**What it is:** Lever is an ATS that combines applicant tracking with candidate relationship management features, often used to nurture talent pipelines.

**What it feels like as a candidate:** Typically one of the simplest experiences: upload a resume, add links, answer a few questions, and submit.

**Where you will see it:** Startups and growth-stage companies, especially in tech.

**Strengths for candidates:**

- Short, low-friction forms
- Easy to apply to many roles quickly

**Watch out for:**

- Resume parsing may pre-fill fields incorrectly, so check them
- Short forms mean less space to explain yourself, so your resume and links carry more weight

**How to win:** Make sure your resume and GitHub or portfolio links tell the full story, because the form will not give you much room.

## Workday

**What it is:** Workday is a large enterprise platform for HR and finance, and its recruiting module is used by many big organizations.

**What it feels like as a candidate:** Longer and more structured. You usually create an account for the employer, upload your resume, and then confirm or re-enter your experience, education, and skills into separate fields. Autofill helps but is rarely perfect.

**Where you will see it:** Large corporations, banks, consulting firms, universities, and global enterprises.

**Strengths for candidates:**

- Once you have an account with an employer, later applications there are quicker
- You can often save drafts and return later
- Structured data makes your profile searchable inside the company

**Watch out for:**

- Account creation for each employer adds friction
- Autofill commonly scrambles dates and titles
- The process can feel long, which tempts people to rush and make errors

**How to win:** Keep a plain text version of your work history ready, set aside real time for the first application, and review the final summary page carefully. See our step-by-step [guide to applying through ATS platforms](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-apply-to-jobs-on-greenhouse-workday-lever-and-workable-a-step-by-step-guide) for a full walkthrough.

You can browse [Workday jobs on GetHired](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/jobs?platform=workday).

## Workable

**What it is:** Workable is an all-in-one hiring platform that includes job posting, applicant tracking, and screening tools, popular with small and mid-size businesses.

**What it feels like as a candidate:** A light form with resume upload, personal details, and screening questions. Some employers add short written or video questions.

**Where you will see it:** Small and mid-size companies, agencies, and startups across many industries.

**Strengths for candidates:**

- Usually fast with no required account
- Clear, simple interface

**Watch out for:**

- Screening questions can include knockout items, like work authorization or years of experience
- Occasional video or timed questions, so read the form before starting

**How to win:** Answer screening questions accurately and read everything first. If there is a video question, prepare a short, clear answer before you begin recording.

You can browse [Workable jobs on GetHired](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/jobs?platform=workable).

## Other ATS platforms you will meet

Beyond the big four, you will see many other systems, and the pattern is usually similar: upload, confirm, answer, submit.

- **Rippling**, often used by growing companies that also run HR on the same platform. [See Rippling jobs](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/jobs?platform=rippling).
- **BambooHR**, common in small and mid-size businesses. [See BambooHR jobs](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/jobs?platform=bamboohr).
- **Jobvite**, used by mid-size and larger employers. [See Jobvite jobs](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/jobs?platform=jobvite).
- **Recruitee**, popular with European companies. [See Recruitee jobs](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/jobs?platform=recruitee).
- **Personio**, widely used by European small and mid-size companies. [See Personio jobs](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/jobs?platform=personio).
- **JazzHR**, aimed at small businesses. [See JazzHR jobs](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/jobs?platform=jazzhr).

## Which ATS should you target?

You cannot choose the tool a company uses, but you can choose where to focus your time.

- **If you want fast, high-volume applications:** Prioritize roles on Greenhouse, Lever, and Workable. They take less time per application.
- **If you want large, stable employers:** Expect to use Workday and plan for longer forms.
- **If you want startups and early-stage teams:** Look at companies using Greenhouse, Lever, Workable, and Rippling.
- **If you want European employers:** Look at Personio and Recruitee listings.

Searching by ATS platform on a job board is a useful trick. It lets you find companies of a certain type and size without guessing.

## Does the ATS change your chances?

Not by itself. The platform is a container. What matters is the same everywhere:

1. **A readable resume.** Single column, standard headings, text-based file. See how to [check your resume for ATS compatibility](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-check-if-your-resume-will-pass-an-ats-in-2026-and-fix-it-in-5-minutes).
1. **Relevant keywords** that match the job description.
1. **Strong answers** to custom questions.
1. **Speed.** Many recruiters review early applicants first, so applying soon after a role is posted can help.
1. **Accuracy.** Mistakes in autofilled fields can hide your experience.

Where the platform does matter is in how much effort each application takes, which affects how many good applications you can send.

## How to be efficient across all of them

- Keep one master resume and one plain text copy of your history.
- Maintain a document of reusable answers for common questions.
- Track every application in a sheet with the platform, date, and status.
- Use [job alerts](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/jobs) to apply early.
- Use AI tools to draft cover letters and application answers, then edit them so they sound like you.

## Frequently asked questions

### What is the difference between Greenhouse and Workday?

Greenhouse is typically used by startups and mid-size tech companies and offers a shorter application, often without an account. Workday is typically used by large enterprises and usually asks you to create an account and fill in detailed work history.

### Is Lever or Greenhouse better for candidates?

Neither is clearly better. Both are generally quick to use. Lever forms tend to be slightly shorter, while Greenhouse forms more often include custom written questions.

### Why is Workday so slow to apply on?

Workday collects structured data such as job titles, dates, education, and skills in separate fields, which makes the process longer. Having a plain text work history ready speeds it up.

### Do I need an account for Workable?

Usually not. Most Workable applications can be completed without creating an account, though employers can configure it differently.

### Can an ATS reject me automatically?

Some employers set knockout questions, such as work authorization or minimum experience, that can screen candidates out. Most systems otherwise store and sort applications for recruiters to review.

### How do I find jobs on a specific ATS?

Check the application URL, or use a job board that lets you filter by platform. On GetHired you can filter listings by platforms like Greenhouse, Workday, and Workable.

### Which ATS has the best candidate experience?

It depends on the employer's setup, but lighter systems like Greenhouse, Lever, and Workable are generally seen as quicker and easier than enterprise systems like Workday.

